        "text": "Polycyclic Heterocycles"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00017",
          "segmentId": "m00297:para:para-00017"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00012",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00297:alt:fig-00012-alt",
              "text": "The structures of quinoline, isoquinoline, indole, purine, quinine (antimalarial), tryptophan (amino acid), and adenine (D N A constituent). The atoms are numbered in quinoline, isoquinoline, indole, and purine."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_09_012.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00018",
          "segmentId": "m00297:para:para-00018"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00013",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00297:alt:fig-00013-alt",
              "text": "Quinoline reacts with bromine and sulfuric acid to form 5-bromoquinoline, 8-bromoquinoline. Isoquinoline reacts with nitric acid and sulfuric acid at zero degree Celsius to form 5-nitroisoquinoline and 8-nitroisoquinoline."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_09_013.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00019",
          "segmentId": "m00297:para:para-00019"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00014",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00297:alt:fig-00014-alt",
              "text": "Indole reacts with bromine, and dioxane at zero degree Celsius to form 3-bromoindole and hydrogen bromide."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_09_014.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00020",
          "segmentId": "m00297:para:para-00020"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00015",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00297:alt:fig-00015-alt",
              "text": "The structure and ball-and-stick model in the electrostatic potential map of purine. The atoms are numbered in the structure. Black, gray, and red spheres represent carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen, respectively."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_24_09_015.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
